#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110260 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "29005c66-9f30-5862-926f-8a09320d404c" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Two Lines in Slope-Intercept Form are Perpendicular"}] :question/id #uuid "4cc37ad7-b382-5b7e-bcda-92b2818c5650" :question/is-example true :question/math-academy-id "e-14359" :question/problem "Which of the following lines is perpendicular to $y= -\\dfrac{2}{5}x-3$?\n\n1. $y = 5x + 1$\n2. $y = \\dfrac{5}{2}x + 3$\n3. $y = -\\dfrac{x}{2} + 5$" :question/worked-solution "For the two lines to be perpendicular, their slopes must multiply to $-1$.\n\nThe line\n\n$$\ny=-\\dfrac{2}{5}x-3\n$$\n\nis in slope-intercept form, and its slope is\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac{2}{5}\n$$\n\nLooking at the answer choices, the line\n\n$$\ny = \\dfrac{5}{2}x + 3\n$$\n\nhas a slope of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5}{2}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the slopes together, we have\n\n$$\n\\left(-\\dfrac{2}{5}\\right) \\cdot \\dfrac{5}{2} = -1\n$$\n\nSince the slopes multiply to $-1$, we conclude that the line\n\n$$\ny = \\dfrac{5}{2}x + 3\n$$\n\nis perpendicular to the given line.\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110261 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6f3d7d62-686b-59c4-9ea1-695c6be3180b" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Unknown Parameters Given Two Perpendicular Lines"}] :question/id #uuid "a53a3337-108b-5129-b203-ff70ce8d0abe" :question/is-example true :question/math-academy-id "e-14362" :question/problem "The lines $y=2x+9$ and $3y+kx = 12$ are perpendicular. What is the value of $k$?" :question/worked-solution "For the two lines to be perpendicular, their slopes must multiply to $-1$.\n\n- The first line is in slope-intercept form, and the slope is the $x$-coefficient. So the slope of the line $y=2x+9$ is $2$.\n- To find the slope of $3y+kx = 12$, we convert to slope-intercept form:\n$3y + kx|= 12; 3y|=-kx + 12; y|=-\\frac{k}{3}x + 4$\nTherefore, the slope of the line $3y +kx = 12$ is $-\\dfrac{k}{3}$.\n\nMultiplying the slopes together, we have\n\n$$\n2 \\cdot\\left(-\\dfrac{k}{3}\\right) = -1\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad k = \\dfrac{3}{2}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110262 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a6fee8d5-e1f7-599d-a74c-abc44d973065" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Equations of Perpendicular Horizontal and Vertical Lines"}] :question/id #uuid "5eca5ef2-627f-5d32-8edb-88e223b11d71" :question/is-example true :question/math-academy-id "e-14365" :question/problem "Find the equation of the line that is perpendicular to $x=2$ and passes through the point $(3,4)$." :question/worked-solution "Since the line $x=2$ is a vertical line, a perpendicular line would need to be horizontal. So, the perpendicular line must take the form $y=k$ for some value of $k$.\n\nThe perpendicular line needs to go through the point $(3,4)$, which has a $y$-coordinate of $4$. So, the perpendicular line is $y=4$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110269 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e732f270-1d6f-5ff8-8792-d9b329ea858d" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Two Lines in Standard Form are Perpendicular"}] :question/id #uuid "b5470454-6a27-5eda-a323-74ce90259f4c" :question/is-example true :question/math-academy-id "e-14734" :question/problem "Which of the following lines is perpendicular to $4x - 2y = 10$?\n\n1. $y = \\dfrac{1}{2}x + 3$\n2. $y = -4x + 1$\n3. $y = -\\dfrac{1}{2}x -5$" :question/worked-solution "For the two lines to be perpendicular, their slopes must multiply to $-1$.\n\nTo find the slope of the given line\n\n$$\n4x - 2y = 10\n$$\n\nwe convert to slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4x - 2y &= 10 \\\\\n- 2y &= -4x + 10 \\\\\ny &= 2x - 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn slope-intercept form, the slope is the $x$-coefficient. So, the slope of the given line is $2$.\n\nLooking at the answer choices, the line\n\n$$\ny = -\\dfrac{1}{2}x -5\n$$\n\nhas a slope of\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the slopes together, we have\n\n$$\n2 \\cdot \\left(-\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right) = -1\n$$\n\nSince the slopes multiply to $-1$, we conclude that the line\n\n$$\ny = -\\dfrac{1}{2}x -5\n$$\n\nis perpendicular to the given line.\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111111 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e732f270-1d6f-5ff8-8792-d9b329ea858d" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Two Lines in Standard Form are Perpendicular"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "892c2c15-da1d-5ddf-a4e4-075f23a6ace5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = 5x - 4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186108762} {:answer/id #uuid "d67c86a9-bc07-525f-a8f3-0eda7891bc11" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y =-\\frac{1}{5}x + 15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186108763} {:answer/id #uuid "ef9c49e1-3b7c-5805-aef2-6bd755a7d14d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y =-x + 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186108764} {:answer/id #uuid "5eb0b61b-101a-5626-80f4-9a0fd998a33c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y =-5x - 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186108765} {:answer/id #uuid "77b98843-90f1-50bd-a72f-744e03b6e785" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = \\frac{1}{5}x - 6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186108766}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "892c2c15-da1d-5ddf-a4e4-075f23a6ace5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = 5x - 4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186108762} :answer-field/id #uuid "44d318db-f5ac-536a-8061-3350c15b7175" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186108761}] :question/id #uuid "f8ebc497-48a2-5b22-a14f-221db06dd5d2" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-44404" :question/problem "Which of the following lines is perpendicular to $x + 5y = 10$?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111112 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e732f270-1d6f-5ff8-8792-d9b329ea858d" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Two Lines in Standard Form are Perpendicular"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1cabb86d-a151-58d0-84be-85758aaf5dd7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = 3x + 8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186108768} {:answer/id #uuid "2d059998-937c-5a19-ab84-1142b1d54688" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y =-3x - 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186108769} {:answer/id #uuid "43e6be3a-90f3-5ce7-800a-5b41dd3fabc0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y =-\\frac{1}{3}x + 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186108770} {:answer/id #uuid "671c4361-05df-572b-9454-c8c9da70ebdb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = \\frac{1}{3}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186108771} {:answer/id #uuid "7ea7ad26-9144-5afc-849d-e21c0aa21f36" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = \\frac{4}{5}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186108772}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "43e6be3a-90f3-5ce7-800a-5b41dd3fabc0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y =-\\frac{1}{3}x + 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186108770} :answer-field/id #uuid "1b3784b4-8208-5f87-8623-3c592c69f666" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186108767}] :question/id #uuid "b7485dae-88b3-5eea-a431-272c46bd98d6" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-44514" :question/problem "Which of the following lines is perpendicular to $12x - 4y = 5$?"}]}
